            "In a serial multi-echelon supply chain, material flows strictly sequentially through a series of N stages: Stage N purchases raw materials from external suppliers, processes them, and transfers them to Stage N minus 1, which in turn feeds Stage N minus 2, down to Stage 1, which directly fulfills external customer demand. Under standard convex holding and backorder penalty cost structures, Clark and Scarf proved that an echelon base-stock policy—often termed an echelon (s, S) policy—is globally optimal for every stage across finite and infinite planning horizons.",
