            "Once Stage 1 is solved, Clark and Scarf introduce the concept of an 'induced penalty cost' that is transferred recursively upstream to Stage 2. When Stage 2 experiences a stockout, Stage 1 does not immediately experience a customer backorder; rather, Stage 1 experiences an increase in effective replenishment lead time or an added probability of future stockouts. By calculating the expected additional shortage and holding costs incurred at Stage 1 as a function of Stage 2's echelon inventory position, the algorithm constructs an induced cost function.",
