            "The primary operational advantage of the Guaranteed Service Model is its extraordinary computational tractability across massive industrial supply chains. Enterprise manufacturers with tens of thousands of SKUs and deep multi-tier bills of materials can solve global GSM optimization models across entire planetary supply networks in minutes. This scalability makes GSM the de facto optimization engine embedded within premier commercial enterprise supply chain planning platforms.",
            "In contrast, the Stochastic Service Model (SSM) rejects the assumption of deterministic service times and bounded demand envelopes. Rooted in queueing network theory and the Clark-Scarf tradition, SSM explicitly models random, stochastic lead times between consecutive supply stages. When an upstream node experiences an inventory shortage, downstream nodes do not receive partial shipments or emergency expediting; rather, downstream replenishment orders sit in queue, causing stochastic delays that cascade down the distribution tree as dynamic lead time variance.",
